WEBVTT

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:21.840
This is the story of Britain, but a Britain we rarely see.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:26.320
Britain as an undiscovered country.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:32.560
Our glorious landscape isn't just spectacular.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:36.280
It's full of secrets and surprises.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:43.400
We asked you to share your secret places.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:47.360
It's absolutely beautiful.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.400
And the response was overwhelming.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:54.480
You're taking us to some remarkable sites.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.240
How bizarre.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.560
I've never climbed anything this high in my life.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:04.120
We'll also share a few secrets of our own.

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:10.640
'I really enjoy discovering secret wild swimming spots.'

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.360
Oh, that's amazing!

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:18.840
'This is Britain as you've never seen it before.'

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.040
Oh, my word!

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:31.800
If you want to know a secret...

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:46.080
Then come with us.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:54.600
The great British weather.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:02.960
Love it or loathe it, the rain keeps our land green and pleasant.

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:05.400
Essential for farmers like me.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:10.160
And for walkers like me... watery wonderlands spring up.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:29.640
But some of our best water features are the most secret.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:33.520
Their charms only known to the fortunate few.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:42.160
Hunting for those idyllic spots, spurred on by your suggestions...

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:47.760
We've come to South Wales and the Brecon Beacons.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:54.640
On the western border of the Beacons, the Black Mountain rises.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:03:01.520
Crowned with a majestic ice age lake.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:04.640
To the east is the glorious ribbon of the River Wye.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:10.360
And in the heart of the Brecon Beacons, a water world of surprises.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:17.200
Waiting to be discovered.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:23.920
Our first watery secrets got us up at first light.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:33.200
I hope it's worth it, it's so early!

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:35.160
It's certainly a beautiful morning.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.840
Yeah, it's good.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:46.520
'We're in search of the fabled Dragons' Breath - 'not fire, but water.'

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:51.720
'It appears just a few times a year.'

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:57.560
'Simon Powell has long hunted for the watery Dragons' Breath.'

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:58.200
Simon, how are you doing?

00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:00.520
Great, thanks.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:07.840
'Thanks to Simon's obsession, 'he's filmed the secrets of the magical vapour, 'which occasionally visits this valley.'

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:08.440
'So we are in for a rare treat.'

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:12.800
Wow, look at that!

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.480
Oh, my goodness!

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:34.000
It's just mist.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:44.520
Myth would have it that the Dragon lives in the cave obviously through the day and then in the night, comes down to the valleys, looking for unsuspecting souls.

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:44.880
And this is his breath after a good meal?

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.520
I love that!

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.160
After a night's hunting.

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:57.360
If you wake up in the morning and you see a good dragon's breath, the dragon's had a good feed and we will all be safe for a wee while!

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:59.680
It just totally absorbs all civilisation.

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:02.160
You sound like you really love being here.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:02.560
Oh, yeah.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:03.200
How can you not?

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.920
Do you feel like it's part of you?

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:06.760
Oh, definitely.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.440
So while we're here, what should we try and see?

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:12.960
If you're brave and you're bold, some of the caving systems around here are spectacular.

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:22.360
And there's a cave which is so far in that it takes a complete day to get in there, and then you camp overnight - why you would want to camp overnight underground in a cave, I never know!

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:25.040
That's one of my biggest fears!

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:27.600
Ellie is little and nimble.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:28.160
I might have a go at that.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:29.000
You can then push over to waterfall country.

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:29.720
That's spectacular.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:32.560
Lots of large waterfalls.

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:33.080
Cascading waterfalls.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:35.680
Llangorse Lake is a must.

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:39.760
You've got to visit Llangorse Lake.

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:42.960
People have been drawn to Llangorse Lake since the beginning of time.

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:47.160
You cut me, I'll bleed Llangorse Lake!

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:50.600
You've set us off on our journey beautifully.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:51.640
I can't wait to get out there and see some of this.

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:54.160
You'll have a great time, you really will.

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:58.560
And it'll stay with you for a long time as well, this little patch of Wales.

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:00.080
Thanks for sharing it with us, Simon.

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:03.520
Enjoy your travels.

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:06.680
Simon is our springboard to aquatic adventure.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:11.560
He's revealed a secret water world where sky is sodden.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:19.640
And land is overrun.

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:21.520
We can't wait to get on the water.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:22.400
And in it!

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:27.040
To explore its untold stories and drink in its secret delights.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:39.840
With so many trips to squeeze in, we're going solo for a while.

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:48.920
I'm making for one of our top tips - Llangorse Lake.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:00.200
This place is very atmospheric.

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:04.240
There's a warm wind and rather strange light.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:07.720
Makes it feel rather magical.

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:13.640
I just want to soak it all in.

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:17.960
But what's so secretive here?

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:34.800
'My guide Mark Redknapp has a passion for the puzzles of this place.'

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:36.320
Mark, where are we headed?

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:41.320
We're headed over to this wooded island here, in actual fact, it's not an island.

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:41.680
An island that's not an island.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:44.000
That's quite a mystery.

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:47.360
People aren't normally allowed to land on it.

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:51.040
But we've been given special permission.

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:52.320
Wow.

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:53.640
What is this place?

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:56.680
We are actually standing on this completely man-made island.

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:58.200
Man-made?

00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:58.480
Wow!

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:06.000
You first lay out bundles of brushwood, you pin those down with oak stakes, timbers like this.

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:07.480
So how old is it?

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:20.640
Well, luckily, we can use tree ring dating to date oak and that tells us that some of the oak, like the one down there, was actually felled between the years 889 and 893 AD.

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:21.680
Incredible.

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:25.480
'A post hammered in place over 1,000 years ago.

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:32.600
'That oak and many more laid the foundations.

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:37.280
'But who built this island from scratch?

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:41.559
'Their secret skills have stood the test of time.

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:55.040
'Now trees have taken over, 'but once this artificial island was crowned with grand wooden houses.'

00:08:54.240 --> 00:09:02.800
The Royal Palace, built around 900 AD, made for a Welsh king.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:08.320
But that King had a powerful English enemy.

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:17.720
Queen Aethelflaed - her Anglo-Saxon warriors laid waste to this royal residence.

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:31.640
Now, 1,000 years on, it's the birds who lord it over the lake.

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:44.320
Water has been at the centre of life here for thousands of years.

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:52.240
If you know where to look, you'll find that it's hiding all kind of secrets.

00:09:56.560 --> 00:10:05.000
'Your suggestions for surprising stories are inspiring Ellie and me.

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:08.920
'As we explore the secret water world of South Wales.

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:11.640
'Whose mountains are massive collectors of moisture.'

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:17.200
Snow and rain saturate the ground.

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:20.400
Water that feeds mighty rivers.

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:24.080
Rivers that run with fish.

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:32.160
And fish attract fishermen.

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:37.840
Landing a prize catch is a closely guarded secret.

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:42.400
Those in the know head to the Brecon Beacons and the River Usk.

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:53.840
Hiding somewhere in these restless waters are some of Britain's best brown trout.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:57.760
Trout with a taste for flies.

00:10:57.560 --> 00:11:02.400
Tempting me to reveal a secret of my own.

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:15.960
I got the bug for fly-fishing as a teenager, and it's all very well having all the right equipment, but what you really need to do is find the best spots to fish in.

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:22.840
Young me struggled to discover those secret spots for a big catch.

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:26.440
But now, I've got renewed motivation.

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:34.960
What I'd love to do is take my 12-year-old son Alfie, who's just getting into fishing, and go off and discover our own little secret places to fish in.

00:11:35.560 --> 00:11:44.960
So I'm hooking up with a master of the mysterious arts, a world championship contender with reel and line, who is well practised at plucking trout from the River Usk...

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:47.800
..Kim Tribe.

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:55.640
I can say to people, "Think like a fish."

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:58.120
How do you think like a fish?

00:11:57.320 --> 00:12:00.600
I'd be out of a business otherwise.

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:05.040
Looking at the water, where do you think the fish might be?

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:05.400
You've got a big conveyor belt bringing food down to the fish.

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:16.280
Aquatic insects, the nymphs from under the stones, dry flies that are hatching out, any insects that fall from the trees.

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:16.520
They are all coming down in that chute of water.

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:22.160
Fish predominantly like to stay on what they call a seam, or a crease, just where it changes speed.

00:12:21.560 --> 00:12:22.760
So it's a little bit slower.

00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:26.080
They'll sit in the slow water, when something comes past them in the fast water, they'll grab it.

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:34.800
The trick is making sure the trout grab our fly rather than a real one.

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:38.720
So Kim is deploying his secret weapon.

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:44.920
I'm going to use a little caddis imitation.

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:45.560
They are quite bright, so you can see it on the top.

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:47.640
You can see it on the top.

00:12:46.840 --> 00:12:52.960
That's what you want to be able to see in low light conditions.

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:58.600
But the fish don't see that yellow bit, the fish sees the underneath of the fly.

00:12:57.720 --> 00:13:03.360
I fished a lot as a boy, but not much lately.

00:13:02.680 --> 00:13:09.000
It's not about distance casting, it's about controlling the line.

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:13.400
The closer you can get to the fish without scaring it, the better.

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:17.160
This is great fun, isn't it?

00:13:16.360 --> 00:13:19.160
But quite tricky.

00:13:18.760 --> 00:13:24.400
Yes, we spooked a couple of fish there, you know.

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:28.600
The water is on its bones at the moment, it's really low.

00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:36.760
If you want to catch a fish in low water conditions, it's tough, so you've got to be able to move around.

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:44.640
I'd be delighted if you can take me to one of your little secret spots.

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:44.880
Would you let me into that?

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:45.360
Will you?

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:50.200
So long as you don't tell anybody else, OK?

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:57.840
Rather surprisingly, we are turning our back on the water.

00:13:56.880 --> 00:14:02.400
I'm no expert, but I didn't expect to find fish in a forest.

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:04.160
This is a secret.

00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:08.120
Is this it?

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:10.040
Oh, no, this isn't it.

00:14:11.240 --> 00:14:15.400
Where now, then?

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:16.520
Follow me.

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:23.400
In between these stones now.

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:24.920
So, has anybody fished this before?

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:30.880
Only me, and only once or twice.

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:33.200
This spot is my last chance.

00:14:32.400 --> 00:14:37.440
So, time to deploy my own secret weapon.

00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:40.000
My childhood fly box.

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:43.160
As a boy, I was so keen on my fishing, I used to make my own flies.

00:14:42.200 --> 00:14:56.120
And in fact, I used to tie the feathers onto the shafts of earrings and send them up to my sister in London who used to sell them to her trendy friends.

00:14:55.280 --> 00:14:58.880
I had a good little business going there.

00:14:58.080 --> 00:15:00.080
Oh, this is quite a fiddle.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:09.960
So, if I can catch one of these little wild brown trout with my own flies, that will be superb.

00:15:09.080 --> 00:15:10.240
Right, there we go.

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:11.920
OK.

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:22.640
It's got to go at the same speed as those in bubbles on the flow and then they'll take it.

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:27.920
Yes, get ready now, whoa, don't pull it.

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:30.880
Hold on, hold on, hold on.

00:15:31.920 --> 00:15:34.040
So, what have we got there?

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:39.280
We've got a little salmon parr here, perfect.

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:39.960
As nature intended.

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:40.200
Yes, on my rod.

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:40.480
Let's have a little hold of it.

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:40.800
OK, so wet hand.

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:41.000
Well, there we are.

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:43.120
A wonderful little young, wild fish.

00:15:49.880 --> 00:15:50.960
So releasing the fish, hold it in the water, face it upstream, let it swim away under its own devices.

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:59.120
Kim, thank you so much for letting me in on your beautiful secret spot.

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:01.160
My pleasure, Adam, my pleasure.

00:16:07.160 --> 00:16:17.280
The spot most people make for in the Brecon Beacons is the highest peak, Pen y Fan.

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:17.800
It's a great view, for sure.

00:16:16.960 --> 00:16:26.000
But this heavenly slice of South Wales has much more in store.

00:16:25.160 --> 00:16:26.960
If you know where to look.

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:31.160
Your tip-offs have set us hunting for hidden gems.

00:16:33.560 --> 00:16:43.160
Special sites squirrelled away in this Welsh land of legend that's soaked in watery secrets.

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:49.800
These hills were polished smooth in the ice age.

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:53.400
When the ice melted, people poured in.

00:16:52.600 --> 00:17:01.080
Ancient folk, folk whose ways we struggle to make sense of.

00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:06.400
This stone has kept their secrets for thousands of years.

00:17:05.599 --> 00:17:11.400
Now, archaeologist Natalie Ward looks after it.

00:17:10.440 --> 00:17:15.960
She's waiting for sun and stone to align.

00:17:16.599 --> 00:17:19.160
And...she's waiting for us.

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:19.319
How are you doing, Natalie?

00:17:20.079 --> 00:17:21.440
Hi.

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:22.880
Good, thanks.

00:17:22.160 --> 00:17:23.800
Wow!

00:17:22.960 --> 00:17:25.359
That's a big lump of stone, isn't it?

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:27.520
It is, it's a whopper.

00:17:27.839 --> 00:17:29.359
What sort of rock is it?

00:17:28.600 --> 00:17:31.600
Old red sandstone.

00:17:30.640 --> 00:17:35.840
Gosh, all these lichens and mosses.

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:38.640
Who put it here?

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:39.960
It's from the late Neolithic or early Bronze Age.

00:17:39.080 --> 00:17:43.560
That's about 4,000 years ago.

00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:43.920
That's a hugely heavy rock.

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:44.640
How on earth did they get it to stand up?

00:17:43.840 --> 00:17:45.160
We don't really know.

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:53.080
It's a proper feat of engineering, but at least a quarter, or third, of it must be below ground, so we're talking a big chunk of rock.

00:17:52.520 --> 00:17:57.280
And what about the obvious question, why?

00:17:57.200 --> 00:17:58.960
Exactly what it meant is sort of lost in the mists of time, really.

00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:07.120
Actually, there's a myth attached to this stone that on midsummer, in the evening, just as the sun is setting, the stone actually moves and goes down to the river to drink.

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:08.880
If you look round, it casts a really long shadow.

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:13.560
We think it's the shadow that goes like a tongue down to the river.

00:18:12.640 --> 00:18:15.360
Look at it, it's the nearly there now.

00:18:14.680 --> 00:18:17.880
Is it lucky if it hits the river or anything like that?

00:18:17.400 --> 00:18:21.480
I've not heard of anything to do with the myth.

00:18:20.560 --> 00:18:25.520
So often with these sorts of sites, there is that sort of connotation.

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:26.000
We can start our own myths, can't we?

00:18:25.040 --> 00:18:27.040
You come into fortune.

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:31.200
I'm going to go down there if it's going to be lucky.

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:37.160
It must be 60 metres from the stream back to the stone.

00:18:37.480 --> 00:18:43.120
I reckon it's on this rock.

00:18:42.280 --> 00:18:45.280
I think we're there, Ellie.

00:18:44.360 --> 00:18:47.120
It's here.

00:18:46.200 --> 00:18:49.120
The stone is now drinking from the stream.

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:52.200
Bringing me luck.

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:57.680
You've been licked by the legend of the luck stone.

00:18:56.720 --> 00:19:02.440
My legend of the luck stone shall endure for another 4,000 years.

00:19:03.760 --> 00:19:10.560
I doubt it.

00:19:19.760 --> 00:19:27.800
In the high country of South Wales, stone and water are surprisingly close companions.

00:19:27.240 --> 00:19:35.160
The tiniest trickle will, over time, erode the rock.

00:19:34.280 --> 00:19:43.000
Feeding rivers carving their course along the valleys.

00:19:42.040 --> 00:19:45.520
Putting on a spectacular show for the tourists.

00:19:44.800 --> 00:19:52.480
But the water also has a secret life few get to see.

00:19:51.520 --> 00:19:54.720
When it seeps deep into the earth.

00:19:56.160 --> 00:20:05.840
I'm going with the flow under the mountains into a cave cut by water.

00:20:06.520 --> 00:20:09.000
To get underground, I'm going to need help.

00:20:08.080 --> 00:20:16.280
Gary Evans is with some members of the South Wales Caving Club.

00:20:15.520 --> 00:20:18.160
I'm hoping they know the way in... and out.

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:20.000
So, where are we heading, then?

00:20:19.040 --> 00:20:20.240
We are heading to this cave.

00:20:19.280 --> 00:20:26.120
This is called Ogof Ffynnon Ddu.

00:20:26.040 --> 00:20:27.480
This is the deepest cave in the UK, just over 300 metres from top to bottom.

00:20:26.520 --> 00:20:27.640
Wow!

00:20:26.840 --> 00:20:28.000
We're going to see a river running through the cave.

00:20:27.120 --> 00:20:33.920
And what's special in here, we're going to see formations called the Bee's Knees.

00:20:33.080 --> 00:20:35.720
And the Ballerina.

00:20:34.880 --> 00:20:41.200
And they are just exquisite, they are really fine formations and very few people have seen them.

00:20:40.280 --> 00:20:41.520
Amazing.

00:20:40.800 --> 00:20:44.640
So we are going into this secret world.

00:20:45.040 --> 00:20:47.640
We've got an over-suit for you.

00:20:46.920 --> 00:20:50.320
That will protect your clothing.

00:20:49.480 --> 00:20:54.160
It'll keep you warm as well.

00:20:53.800 --> 00:21:00.200
I'm not sure "like" is the word, but functional is absolutely right.

00:21:01.600 --> 00:21:08.000
OK.

00:21:10.840 --> 00:21:17.880
Soon, we'll lift the lid on a mysterious, pitch-black water world.

00:21:17.160 --> 00:21:25.600
And they say it's not only what you discover underground, but what you discover about yourself.

00:21:26.680 --> 00:21:29.480
We are unlocking the cave's secrets.

00:21:33.840 --> 00:21:35.760
OK, guys.

00:21:35.240 --> 00:21:36.640
'And one of mine.'

00:21:39.320 --> 00:21:45.440
Perhaps now is a good time to tell you that I'm a bit claustrophobic.

00:21:44.640 --> 00:21:50.640
I even freak out in a tight wetsuit, I'm not even joking.

00:21:49.800 --> 00:21:58.760
I'm not going to see daylight again for 10 hours.

00:21:57.840 --> 00:22:00.440
Wish me luck.

00:21:59.800 --> 00:22:02.920
It's a wall of cold air.

00:22:01.960 --> 00:22:04.880
Oh, I don't like this.

00:22:09.320 --> 00:22:17.880
To make things worse, we are not alone down here.

00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:20.560
If you can hear that, it's running water.

00:22:21.680 --> 00:22:27.520
It sounds like a lot of running water.

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:33.080
'Water with the power to carve this passage through solid rock.

00:22:32.360 --> 00:22:36.280
'What more secrets are in store?'

00:22:35.480 --> 00:22:37.640
Wow, what have we got here?

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:40.720
This looks like a solid waterfall.

00:22:39.840 --> 00:22:45.960
I know, it's all calcites, so it's all limestone that was dissolved and has reformed as calcite.

00:22:45.080 --> 00:22:47.200
But it does look like a waterfall, doesn't it?

00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:48.720
It looks incredible.

00:22:47.920 --> 00:22:49.160
We're going up there.

00:22:48.400 --> 00:22:49.640
It looks treacherous.

00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:50.120
It's not.

00:22:49.240 --> 00:22:50.680
Look how shiny that is.

00:22:49.840 --> 00:22:50.960
Let's get you into there.

00:22:50.120 --> 00:22:51.880
OK, take in.

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:54.040
Grab hold of this side, and pull it there.

00:22:53.200 --> 00:22:56.080
OK, on rope.

00:22:55.240 --> 00:22:57.440
Lines are ready.

00:22:56.480 --> 00:22:59.120
Take the strain.

00:22:58.640 --> 00:22:59.840
OK.

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:00.120
Do a bit of walking up here.

00:23:03.040 --> 00:23:06.240
It looks so slippery, but actually it isn't, is it?

00:23:05.400 --> 00:23:06.800
It's remarkable.

00:23:05.960 --> 00:23:07.400
Sorry.

00:23:11.440 --> 00:23:17.360
Wow, look at that, I'm up, I'm up.

00:23:16.680 --> 00:23:18.000
Hurrah!

00:23:22.560 --> 00:23:24.640
Crikey!

00:23:23.760 --> 00:23:27.840
The environment of the cave has changed completely.

00:23:27.040 --> 00:23:29.720
It has, hasn't it?

00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:37.800
All this water wasn't on the floor before, it's not just dripping down from the ceiling any more, it's gushing down.

00:23:37.080 --> 00:23:38.360
That's right, we're in the main stream here.

00:23:37.400 --> 00:23:42.120
It's a bit low today, but this is taking water from all the way up the cave.

00:23:41.480 --> 00:23:42.680
It can flood over our heads.

00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:46.280
How quickly have you seen it go from that to above head height?

00:23:45.720 --> 00:23:56.320
You wouldn't want to be here when it happens, but I've been here in the morning and it's been fairly low, then come back in the afternoon after heavy rain and it's been right up here somewhere, you can just see it from above.

00:23:55.880 --> 00:23:57.280
Really?

00:23:56.400 --> 00:23:58.720
I know.

00:23:57.880 --> 00:23:59.960
For now, we are the only ones who can see this.

00:24:00.560 --> 00:24:02.840
And the rock has changed as well, it seems very dark now.

00:24:02.080 --> 00:24:03.760
Yes, that gives the cave its name.

00:24:02.880 --> 00:24:11.080
So, Ogof Ffynnon Ddu means "cave of the black spring".

00:24:10.160 --> 00:24:13.120
And the black being this black limestone.

00:24:12.360 --> 00:24:20.720
It's incredible, it feels like a secret world underneath the world.

00:24:26.120 --> 00:24:27.520
I'm in the freak-out zone, Gary.

00:24:26.760 --> 00:24:28.240
You know I hate this.

00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:31.880
It does get a bit smaller here, as you can see.

00:24:31.200 --> 00:24:33.680
Yes.

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:37.080
So we're hands and knees for a little while now.

00:24:36.640 --> 00:24:37.880
You'll be able to drag your bag behind you.

00:24:37.320 --> 00:24:38.640
Right.

00:24:38.840 --> 00:24:39.920
If your bag gets stuck at all, just kick it with your feet.

00:24:43.640 --> 00:24:47.720
I'm better off not even looking at it.

00:24:46.960 --> 00:24:48.440
I'm just going to deal with it as it happens.

00:24:47.880 --> 00:24:52.520
At the moment I'm pretending the sky is just there.

00:24:51.640 --> 00:24:54.400
He makes it look so easy.

00:24:53.440 --> 00:24:58.080
Oh, my goodness, that's way more crawling than I thought.

00:25:03.640 --> 00:25:04.960
'Be brave, Ellie.

00:25:04.240 --> 00:25:08.000
Gather your courage.

00:25:07.160 --> 00:25:10.600
We'll be back.'

00:25:12.920 --> 00:25:33.080
If it's any consolation, up top, things are also taking a turn for the worse.

00:25:35.560 --> 00:25:46.120
The weather here in the Brecon Beacons can change incredibly quickly, with low cloud or mist coming off the tops and wrapping round the hillside like a blanket.

00:25:45.240 --> 00:25:50.560
Low cloud can be a killer for pilots, as you've told us.

00:25:54.240 --> 00:26:03.080
You astonished us with stories of plane crash sites, especially in the Brecon Beacons.

00:26:02.120 --> 00:26:08.240
Wow, this is where a Lancaster bomber crashed during the Second World War.

00:26:07.320 --> 00:26:10.360
This mangled burnt wreckage.

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:12.640
This is part of the engine.

00:26:11.760 --> 00:26:13.680
And then down here, the twisted and bent crankshaft.

00:26:27.360 --> 00:26:34.680
It would have come down with serious impact.

00:26:38.560 --> 00:26:45.280
This is a memorial to the eight young men who lost their lives.

00:26:44.560 --> 00:26:52.960
And the crash site is one of many on the Brecon Beacons, scattered all over the hills.

00:26:52.720 --> 00:27:02.800
It's a grim reminder of how the fog and mist and low cloud can take lives up here.

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:11.680
But, incredibly, there's one man that was in a plane crash up here in the Brecon Beacons and he survived.

00:27:10.960 --> 00:27:15.200
Frank Stokes was an RAF radio operator.

00:27:14.400 --> 00:27:19.960
Aged just 19, he was on a training flight.

00:27:19.280 --> 00:27:24.240
It was January 12th, 1945.

00:27:24.200 --> 00:27:36.440
Now 89, Frank knows the secrets of surviving a collision with these mountains in low cloud.

00:27:36.680 --> 00:27:37.800
Lovely to meet you.

00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:40.480
Hello, nice to meet you too.

00:27:39.720 --> 00:27:43.240
Whereabouts was the crash?

00:27:42.480 --> 00:27:48.440
Well, on a misty day like this, we can't be very precise, but somewhere up along the top there.

00:27:48.880 --> 00:27:51.440
What about the plane?

00:27:50.760 --> 00:27:55.720
Well, in fact, I've got a picture here to show you, there.

00:27:54.840 --> 00:27:58.280
That's where the pilot sits, on this side.

00:27:57.440 --> 00:28:02.000
And the radio operator on the other side.

00:28:01.400 --> 00:28:05.400
So just the two of you in the aircraft.

00:28:04.760 --> 00:28:06.600
Just the two, yes.

00:28:05.640 --> 00:28:13.160
As we took off, there was snow all over the airfield, it was a very cloudy day.

00:28:12.480 --> 00:28:16.520
Did you have any indication that you were going to crash?

00:28:15.600 --> 00:28:23.360
Well, no, the first hint that I got was a change in engine noise.

00:28:22.400 --> 00:28:28.520
And I took my gaze away from what I was doing and looked ahead, that's when I first saw the mountain there.

00:28:27.880 --> 00:28:33.840
And the impact was strong enough to throw the pilot some distance out of the plane.

00:28:35.080 --> 00:28:39.120
What sort of state was he in when you got to him?

00:28:38.160 --> 00:28:39.480
Well, he was in deep unconsciousness.

00:28:38.520 --> 00:28:44.520
He was breathing very heavily and there was blood coming from his nose.

00:28:43.600 --> 00:28:46.680
There was nothing that could be done.

00:28:45.800 --> 00:28:47.360
And you decided to walk off the mountain.

00:28:46.400 --> 00:28:49.160
That was the best thing to do.

00:28:48.360 --> 00:28:53.960
The ground sort of fell away in that direction, so I went.

00:28:53.160 --> 00:28:58.360
Snow everywhere, the like of which I'd never seen before.

00:28:57.400 --> 00:29:12.600
I got to a point, I could see the road from Brecon to Merthyr Tydfil and it was extreme chance that this guy should come around in his car and offered to take me to the hospital.

00:29:11.640 --> 00:29:15.240
I got out about teatime, actually.

00:29:14.440 --> 00:29:22.640
I seem to remember a young nurse coming along and feeding me sausages and mash.

00:29:21.840 --> 00:29:25.960
And they discovered that you had got a broken back.

00:29:25.120 --> 00:29:32.160
It was only later on that an RAF doctor told me that I'd fractured my spine.

00:29:31.320 --> 00:29:33.320
Goodness me!

00:29:32.600 --> 00:29:36.960
And the pilot, sadly, did die later.

00:29:36.320 --> 00:29:39.080
They wouldn't tell me right away.

00:29:38.240 --> 00:29:43.000
I had to ask a couple of times, but then they told me that he died.

00:29:42.120 --> 00:29:45.320
Do you think luck was on your side?

00:29:44.520 --> 00:29:48.480
Oh, it must have been, yes, yes.

00:29:52.480 --> 00:30:00.800
As a teenager, Frank faced a date with destiny on this mountain.

00:30:00.040 --> 00:30:18.480
70 years later, he has three children, 11 grandkids and two great-grandchildren... ..all because Frank lived to tell the tale when lost in the swirling mist of this secret water world.

00:30:17.720 --> 00:30:34.640
Even in clear skies, these mountains conceal watery surprises.

00:30:33.920 --> 00:30:41.000
Some of the rain is stored, but more seeps deep underground.

00:30:41.600 --> 00:30:48.120
That's where I am, Adam, in Britain's deepest cave.

00:30:47.360 --> 00:30:54.400
Passages carved by the irresistible force of water.

00:30:53.520 --> 00:31:03.640
And I'm told tiny water drops have also created amazing formations and crystal jewels few have ever seen.

00:31:02.920 --> 00:31:06.080
Secrets I can't wait to see.

00:31:08.040 --> 00:31:11.240
That's the good news.

00:31:10.400 --> 00:31:14.200
The bad news is everything else.

00:31:13.400 --> 00:31:19.120
Oh, no.

00:31:18.320 --> 00:31:19.680
I hate this, I absolutely hate this.

00:31:18.720 --> 00:31:31.840
I'm happy with spiders, snakes... ..even clowns, but squeeze me through a hole and I'm going to pass out with stress.

00:31:33.960 --> 00:31:38.560
Nearly there.

00:31:39.080 --> 00:31:43.000
Don't like that at all.

00:31:42.720 --> 00:31:43.840
Oh, my God.

00:31:49.360 --> 00:31:53.240
Oh, I hate this.

00:31:56.600 --> 00:32:00.400
Even my bag won't fit through.

00:32:02.760 --> 00:32:09.160
You OK?

00:32:08.280 --> 00:32:09.520
Oh, thank God!

00:32:08.560 --> 00:32:10.160
Gary, I just don't know why you do this.

00:32:14.120 --> 00:32:21.120
I just hate that squeezing.

00:32:20.240 --> 00:32:21.520
Of all the things in all the world, I can cope with so much, but just...

00:32:21.600 --> 00:32:24.440
Do you know why?

00:32:23.560 --> 00:32:26.560
What is it about it...?

00:32:25.840 --> 00:32:35.840
I don't know, it's just innate, it's something deep within me, even just the idea of it actually makes me kind of want to look away, I can't even think about it.

00:32:34.960 --> 00:32:38.040
Well, you see, with practice, it'll get better.

00:32:37.080 --> 00:32:38.240
Good.

00:32:37.280 --> 00:32:39.080
Shall we practise?

00:32:38.720 --> 00:32:39.880
I suppose we should go on.

00:32:39.040 --> 00:32:42.600
Yeah, we should head on.

00:32:41.720 --> 00:32:45.320
No more of those, thank you very much.

00:32:47.520 --> 00:32:52.560
Knee up onto this one?

00:32:54.640 --> 00:33:06.840
'Thankfully, as the passage opens up, after one last struggle, 'we're close to our watery secrets, 'known to cavers as the Ballerina and the Bee's Knees.

00:33:06.120 --> 00:33:10.520
'I hope my legs hold out.'

00:33:10.080 --> 00:33:11.640
Whoa, steady there.

00:33:11.480 --> 00:33:16.960
S Just ahead of us here, this should be worth looking at.

00:33:16.280 --> 00:33:17.920
Oh, wow.

00:33:16.960 --> 00:33:21.280
Why so white like that?

00:33:20.520 --> 00:33:25.680
It's all just pure calcium carbonate.

00:33:24.880 --> 00:33:28.520
They're almost see-through, aren't they?

00:33:27.680 --> 00:33:30.440
So that's the Bee's Knees?

00:33:29.480 --> 00:33:30.560
S Yes.

00:33:29.680 --> 00:33:33.240
Is that why it's called the Bee's Knees?

00:33:32.440 --> 00:33:34.600
Cos it's the shape of a knee!

00:33:33.760 --> 00:33:36.720
Exactly right.

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:37.400
And the Ballerina?

00:33:36.480 --> 00:33:40.400
Just step forward and look back at it again.

00:33:39.640 --> 00:33:41.280
Oh, yeah.

00:33:41.600 --> 00:33:44.280
That's the Ballerina.

00:33:43.320 --> 00:33:44.880
Really serene.

00:33:45.000 --> 00:33:46.720
Gosh, in this secret place.

00:33:46.280 --> 00:33:49.040
Gosh, aren't they incredible?

00:33:48.200 --> 00:33:53.640
'The drip-drip-drop of eternity.

00:34:02.040 --> 00:34:03.760
'Water working in secret.

00:34:02.960 --> 00:34:14.040
'Each drop leaves a minuscule deposit of calcium... '..the same mineral that builds our own bones.'

00:34:16.719 --> 00:34:26.320
When we're all gone and the cave's quiet, they're just sat there, quietly doing what they do.

00:34:27.440 --> 00:34:28.600
And there's more to see?

00:34:27.719 --> 00:34:29.880
There's more to see.

00:34:29.199 --> 00:34:31.800
Shall we keep going?

00:34:30.960 --> 00:34:33.320
Yep.

00:34:36.840 --> 00:34:40.199
Oh, my word, Gary, this is something else!

00:34:39.360 --> 00:34:44.080
Looks like snow all over the roof the cave.

00:34:44.199 --> 00:34:48.600
And sparkling as well.

00:34:47.760 --> 00:34:49.080
I know.

00:34:48.120 --> 00:34:49.719
Wow, what is it?

00:34:49.000 --> 00:34:53.679
This is gypsum.

00:34:52.840 --> 00:34:57.480
'Crystal gems glistening like jewels.

00:34:56.520 --> 00:35:05.680
'Hidden treasure of this Welsh water world, precious few have ever seen.'

00:35:04.920 --> 00:35:13.040
It's just here in secret all the time until we come along and throw these lights up, and the whole place just sparkles.

00:35:12.280 --> 00:35:18.120
There have been parts of the journey where I wasn't sure, BUT...it was worth it.

00:35:17.920 --> 00:35:20.480
That's good.

00:35:19.680 --> 00:35:23.920
S Even the squeezes.

00:35:23.200 --> 00:35:25.840
It's made it worth it.

00:35:25.720 --> 00:35:30.000
Now just the small matter of finding my way out.

00:35:42.920 --> 00:35:48.600
In the Brecon Beacons, it's water, water, everywhere.

00:35:47.760 --> 00:35:53.520
Even the odd drop to drink.

00:35:52.680 --> 00:35:58.000
This natural pool was here long before it was dammed.

00:35:58.560 --> 00:36:07.240
It's known as Llyn y Fan Fach, a lake steeped in mythology.

00:36:06.360 --> 00:36:14.600
Yes, the tragic tale of a beautiful lady born from this water and betrayed by a man.

00:36:13.720 --> 00:36:19.440
These showcase sites and their stories captivate allcomers.

00:36:18.480 --> 00:36:19.680
But we're delving deeper.

00:36:25.560 --> 00:36:36.000
We're told this dense thicket conceals a dark secret for us to discover.

00:36:35.320 --> 00:36:40.080
Ah, the legendary Witches' Pool of Pwll-y-Wrach.

00:36:39.240 --> 00:36:43.080
WELSH Yes, Ellie, the Witches' Pool.

00:36:42.200 --> 00:36:45.240
That's not a bad accent, actually!

00:36:44.360 --> 00:36:46.160
Are you?

00:36:45.320 --> 00:36:48.120
Yeah, on my mother's side.

00:36:47.400 --> 00:36:53.000
Well, I thought you were Scottish with the red hair.

00:36:53.160 --> 00:36:58.280
I didn't see any goblins.

00:36:57.480 --> 00:36:59.000
I was glad to have got there, but very glad to have got out.

00:36:58.720 --> 00:37:01.280
You'll have a go next time.

00:37:00.400 --> 00:37:01.680
Hmm.

00:37:01.960 --> 00:37:06.240
Well, we're looking for a pool apparently.

00:37:05.280 --> 00:37:08.840
Yeah, and there's a waterfall by it.

00:37:08.040 --> 00:37:10.960
Well, here's the water.

00:37:10.160 --> 00:37:12.360
Ah, look at this.

00:37:11.520 --> 00:37:15.120
These weird faces in the tress.

00:37:17.040 --> 00:37:20.480
Whoa, that suggests the dark art of the occult, doesn't it?

00:37:20.440 --> 00:37:22.600
They look a bit modern to me.

00:37:22.320 --> 00:37:23.400
Yeah.

00:37:22.600 --> 00:37:23.920
And that's not much of a waterfall.

00:37:23.080 --> 00:37:24.280
I can't believe this is it.

00:37:23.320 --> 00:37:28.400
There must be some more clues somewhere.

00:37:27.800 --> 00:37:33.800
Goodness, look at this.

00:37:33.000 --> 00:37:41.320
Have you found anything?

00:37:40.560 --> 00:37:43.840
A phone box, does that help?

00:37:43.000 --> 00:37:46.400
What?!

00:37:47.320 --> 00:37:56.360
Look at this, no phone but loads of intel.

00:37:55.440 --> 00:38:07.800
'Following the tip-off from the telephone box, 'we're on track for the Witches' Pool.'

00:38:06.960 --> 00:38:14.200
Swirling with a dark secret, fed by a waterfall.

00:38:14.520 --> 00:38:18.680
This must be it, Ellie.

00:38:18.360 --> 00:38:22.120
It's beautiful, isn't it?

00:38:21.360 --> 00:38:23.280
It really is.

00:38:23.640 --> 00:38:27.080
Lovely, rusty red colour all down the waterfall.

00:38:26.120 --> 00:38:29.000
Yeah, beautiful sandstone.

00:38:28.120 --> 00:38:30.640
Not much light, though, is there?

00:38:30.480 --> 00:38:31.560
No, it's quite enclosed.

00:38:31.720 --> 00:38:42.720
It's Welsh name is Pwll-y-Wrach - "Pool of the Witches".

00:38:41.800 --> 00:38:54.160
So the dark legends are that this pool was used to try witches, they would have been drowned because of their craft.

00:38:54.800 --> 00:39:03.480
I was going to go for a swim in that pond, but, um...not sure the mood is right.

00:39:05.240 --> 00:39:10.960
'I could well believe it was a witches' pool.

00:39:10.800 --> 00:39:13.880
'We'll never know for sure.

00:39:15.600 --> 00:39:20.160
'But what a beautiful sight for such a heartbreaking secret.'

00:39:19.400 --> 00:39:25.760
It's spurred me on to find a more suitable spot for a wild swim.

00:39:25.080 --> 00:39:32.040
Not a witches' pool, no, I've got a bit of a hike, but at the end of my walk there's the promise of finding an enchanted fairy pool.

00:39:38.840 --> 00:39:46.760
While you're away with the fairies, Ellie, I'm on my own amazing journey.

00:39:45.920 --> 00:39:52.680
For a farmer like me, it's no surprise to be in a field.

00:39:51.760 --> 00:39:53.200
But just you wait.

00:39:52.440 --> 00:39:59.880
Unlikely as it seems, hidden in these trees are old farmsteads.

00:39:58.920 --> 00:40:02.080
Apparently, cattle grazed here for centuries.

00:40:01.120 --> 00:40:11.080
But where?

00:40:10.160 --> 00:40:15.640
A traditional way of life's been lost.

00:40:14.800 --> 00:40:18.840
The evidence lies in secret somewhere in these woods.

00:40:18.200 --> 00:40:22.800
I want to uncover one family's forgotten story that's generations old.

00:40:22.000 --> 00:40:26.160
And there's some clues over here.

00:40:25.320 --> 00:40:28.360
That wall is definitely man-made.

00:40:28.680 --> 00:40:37.160
Ah, this is what I'm looking for.

00:40:36.320 --> 00:40:44.960
This is one of the old farmsteads that was here before the wood was planted.

00:40:44.040 --> 00:40:51.120
And now it's tumbled down, and the bracken and thorn and bushes have taken over.

00:40:52.360 --> 00:40:58.200
But it's got great big thick walls.

00:40:58.000 --> 00:41:02.160
This looks like it would have been quite a big room in here.

00:41:01.400 --> 00:41:12.760
Before the trees were here, of course, this was all grassland with grazing animals, they would have planted their own vegetables, and it wasn't all that long ago.

00:41:12.680 --> 00:41:18.520
The trees were only planted late sixties, early seventies, so they've grown up very fast, and this old farmstead has just disappeared into the undergrowth.

00:41:25.560 --> 00:41:34.480
Nature has forgotten the farm, but, fortunately, a few people do remember.

00:41:33.560 --> 00:41:40.800
Edwina Cartwright carries those memories from a century ago with her.

00:41:39.960 --> 00:41:42.520
Great to meet you.

00:41:41.760 --> 00:41:49.000
'Her mother's told Edwina the secrets of life on this old farm.'

00:41:48.160 --> 00:41:49.400
So remote, isn't it?

00:41:48.440 --> 00:41:49.520
So who've you got there?

00:41:48.600 --> 00:41:50.840
That's my mother.

00:41:49.920 --> 00:41:52.760
How old do you think she was then?

00:41:51.920 --> 00:41:54.440
She was, I think, 10, 11.

00:41:53.520 --> 00:41:56.720
She was living here at the time.

00:41:55.920 --> 00:41:58.720
So she'd have lived in the house here?

00:41:58.040 --> 00:41:59.440
Yeah.

00:41:58.600 --> 00:42:00.840
Incredible, isn't it?

00:42:00.040 --> 00:42:01.480
I know.

00:42:00.720 --> 00:42:02.880
And my grandmother.

00:42:02.080 --> 00:42:05.040
My word, what an outfit.

00:42:04.240 --> 00:42:06.400
Gorgeous, isn't it?

00:42:05.480 --> 00:42:06.560
So slim as well.

00:42:05.720 --> 00:42:08.320
My grandfather.

00:42:07.560 --> 00:42:10.200
What's his name?

00:42:09.280 --> 00:42:12.520
Christmas.

00:42:11.800 --> 00:42:13.440
Christmas?!

00:42:12.720 --> 00:42:14.040
Yeah.

00:42:13.080 --> 00:42:14.920
What a great name.

00:42:14.000 --> 00:42:15.320
Yeah.

00:42:15.400 --> 00:42:21.160
We're quite remote, they'd have had to run everything on candlelight and those sorts of things.

00:42:20.320 --> 00:42:26.880
There was an outside toilet, and I think they had to get water from the stream.

00:42:25.920 --> 00:42:30.560
Make their own bread, their own butter.

00:42:29.720 --> 00:42:42.040
My auntie and my uncle used to go over on a horse and cart to Penderyn, on to Aberdare, to sell milk, in churns then.

00:42:41.920 --> 00:42:46.120
And how old is your mother now?

00:42:45.160 --> 00:42:47.560
She's 91 in October.

00:42:46.760 --> 00:42:47.880
And does she still talk about the place?

00:42:47.320 --> 00:42:49.200
Oh, yeah.

00:42:48.240 --> 00:42:50.160
She'd love to come up here herself, but...

00:42:50.160 --> 00:42:52.960
Well, it was a job for me getting here!

00:42:52.160 --> 00:42:56.640
Can understand why a 91-year-old wouldn't be able to make it.

00:42:58.640 --> 00:43:06.000
'85 years ago, Edwina's mum discovered her own secret spots 'in a magical landscape, as she began to explore.'

00:43:05.160 --> 00:43:12.880
She used to walk to school, across the river and down into Pontneddfechan.

00:43:12.040 --> 00:43:13.960
How long was that?

00:43:13.120 --> 00:43:16.320
I think four miles.

00:43:16.240 --> 00:43:17.400
Goodness me, there and back every day.

00:43:16.440 --> 00:43:20.880
Rain, hail or shine.

00:43:20.040 --> 00:43:24.200
Come on, let's go and retrace her steps and see which way she would have gone.

00:43:23.320 --> 00:43:24.800
OK.

00:43:24.960 --> 00:43:31.520
'Edwina's mum, when she was just six years old, often set off alone.

00:43:30.680 --> 00:43:38.160
'A wonderful walk, but a challenge for those young legs.'

00:43:37.320 --> 00:43:41.080
My word, Edwina, that's a serious river to cross!

00:43:40.120 --> 00:43:41.240
How did she get through here?

00:43:40.800 --> 00:43:44.960
When it rains, it rises very quickly.

00:43:44.040 --> 00:43:45.760
Making it even more difficult.

00:43:45.240 --> 00:43:46.720
Yeah, yeah.

00:43:46.760 --> 00:43:58.880
'But the secret path to cross the river took that little girl 'to a breathtaking sight.'

00:44:07.440 --> 00:44:13.840
Memories of this glorious waterfall still sparkle for Edwina, and her mum.

00:44:13.040 --> 00:44:23.560
There's a path right behind the waterfall, and that's how she travelled, she walked behind.

00:44:22.600 --> 00:44:32.280
My grandfather used to go behind there on a horse, and he'd take cattle and sheep.

00:44:31.360 --> 00:44:35.200
The only way to cross this valley.

00:44:34.440 --> 00:44:36.120
Let's go and take a look.

00:44:35.320 --> 00:44:41.320
My word!

00:44:41.440 --> 00:44:49.200
It's absolutely beautiful, isn't it?

00:44:48.600 --> 00:44:49.800
I know.

00:44:48.960 --> 00:44:50.320
It's gorgeous.

00:44:49.360 --> 00:44:53.880
With the sun shining through.

00:44:53.160 --> 00:44:54.280
Yeah.

00:44:53.880 --> 00:44:56.480
To think your mum used to do this in her school clothes every day!

00:44:55.720 --> 00:44:57.640
Yeah.

00:44:57.640 --> 00:45:01.240
I'm not sure I want to ride a horse through here.

00:45:00.360 --> 00:45:01.720
Oh, no.

00:45:00.760 --> 00:45:05.760
I suppose after a while the livestock and horses would have got used to it.

00:45:05.360 --> 00:45:06.880
Yeah.

00:45:05.920 --> 00:45:24.040
It's called Sgwd-yr-Eira, and it means "falling of the snow".

00:45:26.200 --> 00:45:27.800
So thank you so much for sharing your family secrets, it's been wonderful, and please send my love to your mum.

00:45:40.520 --> 00:45:52.200
I will, yeah.

00:45:51.440 --> 00:45:53.680
Your secret stories...

00:45:56.000 --> 00:46:03.360
Take us to special places.

00:46:02.760 --> 00:46:07.560
This isn't the only watery short cut we've heard about.

00:46:09.720 --> 00:46:17.200
A secret tale from the banks of the River Wye sparked our interest.

00:46:19.000 --> 00:46:23.280
Crossing this great divide put us in contact with a couple who had a story to share.

00:46:22.560 --> 00:46:24.760
I'm Maggie.

00:46:23.880 --> 00:46:26.680
And I'm Graham.

00:46:25.960 --> 00:46:30.000
And our secret is... ..we bought a bridge!

00:46:29.480 --> 00:46:33.440
Why DID we decide to buy this bridge, Graham?

00:46:32.640 --> 00:46:35.440
Well, it was over lunch.

00:46:36.280 --> 00:46:41.440
I said, "We're going to buy that."

00:46:41.200 --> 00:46:42.280
Your reaction?

00:46:41.480 --> 00:46:46.760
"Oh, no, not another one of your harebrained schemes."

00:46:46.120 --> 00:46:48.240
We fell in love with it.

00:46:47.520 --> 00:46:50.400
Something different in our lives.

00:46:49.600 --> 00:46:50.680
Absolutely.

00:46:50.680 --> 00:46:53.640
Let me introduce you to the bridge, our beautiful bridge.

00:46:52.680 --> 00:47:02.200
It was built here in 1774, the secret route between England and Wales.

00:47:01.320 --> 00:47:10.920
The bridge is definitely a secret to a lot of people, and we find that when they arrive and say, "But the sat nav brought me here.

00:47:10.000 --> 00:47:12.360
I didn't know this existed."

00:47:11.480 --> 00:47:18.080
Over two centuries old, the bridge has been bypassed by new roads.

00:47:20.560 --> 00:47:22.440
Now it's a secret short cut, but it isn't a free ride.

00:47:21.520 --> 00:47:29.600
For unlimited crossings by car, it's just 80p a day.

00:47:28.640 --> 00:47:33.440
80 pence, thank you very much indeed, and that's the return...

00:47:32.480 --> 00:47:35.760
Money that pays to keep the bridge going.

00:47:34.840 --> 00:47:38.800
Good afternoon, how are you doing today?

00:47:37.920 --> 00:47:46.400
Parliament grants the bridge owners the right to charge, but not to charge what they like.

00:47:45.680 --> 00:47:53.920
For us to increase the prices of the toll requires us to get an Act of Parliament passed.

00:47:53.200 --> 00:48:02.520
So it would have to be a very significant increase for us to make it worthwhile to apply for that, which we have no plans for.

00:48:01.680 --> 00:48:05.120
Hello there, that's just 80 pence, please.

00:48:04.160 --> 00:48:05.360
There you go.

00:48:04.400 --> 00:48:06.280
There's a steady stream of income.

00:48:06.960 --> 00:48:10.440
But it's not all plain sailing making a bridge your home.

00:48:09.560 --> 00:48:14.800
We've had probably four, five floods?

00:48:13.920 --> 00:48:15.360
Each year!

00:48:14.400 --> 00:48:15.560
Yeah, since we've been here.

00:48:14.760 --> 00:48:21.720
But it doesn't come into the house, fortunately, it's just very damp.

00:48:21.000 --> 00:48:23.120
And the biggest issue is the debris we catch on the bridge.

00:48:22.240 --> 00:48:30.280
We have had some very large trees wedged on, we then need to get guys with chainsaws out to cut them up for us.

00:48:29.480 --> 00:48:35.840
You never know what's going to flow under your bridge.

00:48:34.960 --> 00:48:42.560
But it's a constant joy to relax and soak in the secret life of the river.

00:48:49.760 --> 00:48:59.480
We had wanted to move to the sea, and we'd been talking about getting nearer to the water, and we said, "Well, there's more water here than we could ever want."

00:48:58.680 --> 00:49:01.960
What a beautiful spot.

00:49:01.520 --> 00:49:10.120
Particularly the views from the bridge upstream are just out of this world.

00:49:10.920 --> 00:49:15.040
Living near the River Wye is just magical.

00:49:15.360 --> 00:49:21.480
We're on the trail of hidden, secret stories.

00:49:22.920 --> 00:49:33.400
It's a quest that's brought us to South Wales and the Brecon Beacons, a realm of watery delights.

00:49:37.480 --> 00:49:49.040
Right now, I'm on a one-man mission to immerse myself completely in this watery world - the best way I know.

00:49:49.920 --> 00:49:57.640
Since we're sharing secrets, one of mine is that when I was growing up I wanted to be the youngest person to swim the English Channel.

00:49:56.800 --> 00:50:06.000
I didn't actually do it in the end, but I still love swimming, and now I really enjoy discovering secret wild swimming spots.

00:50:05.040 --> 00:50:19.760
What makes the lake that I'm going to so special is that it's a watery gateway to another world, and who can resist a legend of the lake?

00:50:18.840 --> 00:50:20.280
Not this lady?

00:50:19.440 --> 00:50:20.880
Ho-ho!

00:50:25.360 --> 00:50:28.920
Wow!

00:50:31.440 --> 00:50:39.560
It's so unexpected, walking all the way up this mountain, you don't even see it until you're right on top of it.

00:50:38.920 --> 00:50:42.560
SHE SIGHS Looks glorious.

00:50:42.040 --> 00:50:53.120
There's always something quite special about water that's collected high up, because obviously it always floats downhill.

00:50:52.160 --> 00:50:59.280
So to have this secret pool hidden from the world... gives it a real magical quality.

00:50:58.440 --> 00:51:04.040
'I'm not the only one enchanted by this fairy-tale pool.'

00:51:03.160 --> 00:51:04.480
How are you doing, Neil?

00:51:03.560 --> 00:51:05.880
Hi there, Ellie, how are you?

00:51:05.040 --> 00:51:06.080
I'm good.

00:51:05.240 --> 00:51:06.520
Have a seat.

00:51:05.840 --> 00:51:06.920
Thank you.

00:51:10.120 --> 00:51:11.960
Cor, what a spot!

00:51:11.160 --> 00:51:18.560
'Believe it or not, 'there's a magical rock sitting in the middle of this lake.

00:51:17.600 --> 00:51:24.920
'Sadly, we can' see it, because, of course, being a fairy kingdom, 'the rock's invisible.

00:51:24.200 --> 00:51:32.920
'I have conjured up man of mythology Neil Thomas to tell me the tale.'

00:51:32.360 --> 00:51:47.800
On the first crow of the cockerel on each May Day, the rocks would cleave apart to reveal a magical tunnel which took anybody who wanted to go on to the kingdom of the fairies, and see the great beauty of the place.

00:51:47.040 --> 00:51:52.920
But a local man came to see the fairies, but his wife was very ill at home and she didn't come.

00:51:51.960 --> 00:51:56.440
And he thought he would take some flowers back for her from the fairy kingdom.

00:51:55.680 --> 00:52:02.840
So he left, taking with him some flowers, and the entrance sealed behind him...

00:52:02.400 --> 00:52:05.440
They never opened again.

00:52:04.520 --> 00:52:07.560
And that's because he took the flowers, even though he was not supposed to.

00:52:06.760 --> 00:52:09.920
That's a sad story.

00:52:08.960 --> 00:52:15.880
It is in a way, but what an ideal place to have a fairy kingdom.

00:52:15.800 --> 00:52:19.160
Exactly, this is where it would be.

00:52:18.320 --> 00:52:19.520
That makes me want to swim even more now.

00:52:18.640 --> 00:52:28.400
Neil slips away as silently as he appeared, leaving me alone.

00:52:30.640 --> 00:52:39.520
This place is amazing, so it's time for my own magical transformation, into a wetsuit.

00:52:38.680 --> 00:52:42.520
'Tiptoeing into the fairy kingdom, 'you soon discover it has its own guardians.'

00:52:42.760 --> 00:52:44.680
Oh, there are leeches everywhere!

00:52:45.920 --> 00:52:51.120
There's a big one and loads of little ones.

00:52:50.200 --> 00:52:52.400
I'm going to get in quicker.

00:52:51.720 --> 00:52:57.000
I've been told it's 27ft deep in the middle, so there's plenty of room for the fairies to hide.

00:52:56.120 --> 00:52:57.600
Oh, my goodness, it's really slippy.

00:52:56.800 --> 00:53:00.840
I'm just going to have to launch, that's the only way over the slippy bit.

00:52:59.880 --> 00:53:00.960
Agh!

00:53:06.440 --> 00:53:09.360
Oh, that's amazing!

00:53:08.680 --> 00:53:13.560
This is awesome.

00:53:13.440 --> 00:53:16.360
Like being a child again.

00:53:22.040 --> 00:53:27.600
This is unlike any place I've ever been before.

00:53:26.880 --> 00:53:33.720
Totally secluded, and the mountains just bear down on you as you swim.

00:53:32.960 --> 00:53:41.880
For this small snapshot of time, it's been my own secret, magical world.

00:53:59.600 --> 00:54:07.720
We've been soaking in surprising stories from a secret water world.

00:54:06.760 --> 00:54:08.480
It's so rich in treasures...

00:54:07.640 --> 00:54:11.960
We're struggling to sum them all up.

00:54:11.080 --> 00:54:14.400
So we're stepping out of the water...

00:54:13.440 --> 00:54:18.480
And into the fire.

00:54:21.880 --> 00:54:23.160
With a man who's here to inspire.

00:54:22.320 --> 00:54:28.400
If you're looking for someone who's good with words, who better to ask than a poet?

00:54:27.720 --> 00:54:33.360
A poet who loves this landscape and its secret delights.

00:54:32.400 --> 00:54:37.840
Christopher Meredith lives here...

00:54:37.080 --> 00:54:40.000
And writes here.

00:54:39.520 --> 00:54:45.840
So we're taking this man of words aloft...

00:54:44.920 --> 00:54:53.560
For a poet's-eye view... ..of this magical, mythical kingdom.

00:54:52.760 --> 00:54:54.800
The heat from the burner is just extraordinary.

00:54:54.040 --> 00:54:55.880
Incredible, isn't it?

00:55:00.120 --> 00:55:03.440
This is a first for me, in a balloon.

00:55:02.520 --> 00:55:04.400
Fairly terrified.

00:55:03.520 --> 00:55:08.440
I like things with engines.

00:55:09.040 --> 00:55:13.480
Farmer's-eye view from up here.

00:55:12.640 --> 00:55:18.880
Sheepdog's going mad down there now.

00:55:18.040 --> 00:55:21.960
It's bringing the sheep in!

00:55:21.080 --> 00:55:23.760
How high up are we, skipper?

00:55:23.000 --> 00:55:24.600
4,500ft.

00:55:23.800 --> 00:55:26.440
That's...

00:55:25.680 --> 00:55:30.400
Just an old wicker basket and a balloon.

00:55:29.560 --> 00:55:34.240
Don't say that.

00:55:34.400 --> 00:55:39.120
So every time we look down we're just getting this feeling about the place.

00:55:38.160 --> 00:55:42.120
You can see why it's inspired so many people, it must inspire you, Chris.

00:55:41.320 --> 00:55:54.280
It does, yes, and last March I was down there with my son, we were looking at the peat bogs on the top there, and we got completely lost in some of this cloud, in a landscape that we know really well.

00:55:53.480 --> 00:55:58.480
I think we made an eight-mile walk into a 20-mile walk by getting lost.

00:55:57.680 --> 00:56:07.920
Somebody I was with one day picked up a Neolithic arrowhead from the top of the ridge, so somebody walked along that ridge 5,000 years ago.

00:56:07.040 --> 00:56:08.200
Man, this is a fantastic place to live.

00:56:07.240 --> 00:56:15.120
Chris, your poetry is about this area, this seems like the best place to hear some.

00:56:14.160 --> 00:56:21.800
Well, this is a poem about the Black Mountains, and it's about the ridge that's just cloaked in cloud to the north of us there.

00:56:21.040 --> 00:56:39.720
Taking colour from those clouds that blow across the sun Falling and swelling to where that edge of upland bites the sky Goes home Resolves at last to almost silence In white noise of living air.

00:56:40.240 --> 00:56:46.480
Goodness me, the hairs have stood up on the back of my neck!

00:56:46.040 --> 00:56:49.600
Floating in the sky, you can just savour that moment.

00:56:48.760 --> 00:56:50.800
Yeah.

00:57:00.000 --> 00:57:08.000
Actually, travelling this slowly over the landscape you can contemplate the pattern.

00:57:07.480 --> 00:57:16.120
Human beings are makers of patterns, and also, to make a poem is to make a pattern.

00:57:15.240 --> 00:57:16.800
And so it connects with that idea too.

00:57:15.960 --> 00:57:26.920
As a farmer, it's really interesting looking down and seeing the patterns of the fields, with round bales and sheep, cultivated land down in the valleys, and then up into forest and these wild, desolate hills.

00:57:27.080 --> 00:57:28.800
I'm going to bring the tone down, I've got a poem now.

00:57:27.880 --> 00:57:39.680
There once was a farmer from Leeds Who swallowed a packet of seeds It soon came to pass He was covered in grass But has all the tomatoes he needs!

00:57:38.720 --> 00:57:43.040
A wonderful limerick!

00:57:43.400 --> 00:57:48.520
What a journey we've enjoyed.

00:57:50.720 --> 00:57:55.920
Your suggestions for your special secret sites...

00:57:55.600 --> 00:57:57.560
Haven't let us down.

00:57:56.720 --> 00:57:59.040
We've been inspired.

00:57:58.320 --> 00:58:03.880
And admired a wonderful Welsh water world.

00:58:03.600 --> 00:58:10.640
We've got to land yet.

00:58:14.920 --> 00:58:15.960
With all our hot air, we might stay up here for a lot longer.

00:58:48.920 --> 00:58:50.160
'The German war is at an end.'

00:58:56.680 --> 00:58:58.920
Churchill was a powerful leader, you were there right behind him.

00:58:58.080 --> 00:58:59.560
'Today is Victory in Europe Day.'

00:59:00.920 --> 00:59:02.160
The war is over, we're going to enjoy it now.

00:59:01.960 --> 00:59:03.320
Let's have a party.

00:59:07.240 --> 00:59:08.720
I don't know why I wasn't rushing around, kissing soldiers, but I wasn't!

00:59:09.200 --> 00:59:10.440
My father came back from the war as a superstar.

00:59:09.480 --> 00:59:11.040
We were free.

00:59:10.080 --> 00:59:13.360
To mark one of the most important days in British history...

00:59:19.920 --> 00:59:21.000
Floated something in Jude's brain.

00:59:20.480 --> 00:59:23.320
You're on a voyage of discovery during the programme.

00:59:22.640 --> 00:59:24.440
Won't be many minutes now before we can start the count.

00:59:23.480 --> 00:59:25.200
It is.

00:59:28.760 --> 00:59:30.200
Peter...

00:59:29.240 --> 00:59:30.520
Let me introduce our new BBC swingometer for the '90s.

00:59:35.680 --> 00:59:36.720
It almost feels like you're in the mind of Alexander McQueen.

00:59:36.560 --> 00:59:37.640
And the music is the thing that tones everything down and keeps everything a little bit peaceful in this cacophony of madness.

00:59:38.400 --> 00:59:39.840
We're an island now, cut off by water and fog.

00:59:38.880 --> 00:59:40.800
These floods have changed us.

00:59:39.840 --> 00:59:41.600
Floated something in Jude's brain.

00:59:40.840 --> 00:59:41.920
Drowned something in mine.
